Reference: onFail only clears watch.stop, but Node has already closed the fs.watch handle when it emits 'error' (bootstrap.ts:182-183), so nothing stays alive. Port: FSEventsFolderWatcher reports RootChanged as onFail, and the stream stays running. FolderWatches.start's onFail closure sets `watch.stop = nil` without calling the old stop closure. The stream (FSEventStreamStart, a retained Context and a DispatchQueue) is never stopped or released, because the only handle to it is dropped. When the window next gets focus, checkForMissedChanges/watch() calls start() again and creates a second stream for the same folder. The first one keeps calling noteChange for the same Watch.

Failing test (a copy is at /private/tmp/claude-416442273/-Users-tcmudemirhan-src-swiftproj-filetrail/f7591ed8-c2ad-47ef-8486-ca4050ceafa7/scratchpad/ProbeFailTests.swift.txt; copy it to Tests/FTFileSystemTests/Watch/ProbeFailTests.swift):
```swift
final class ProbeCounter: @unchecked Sendable { var live = 0 }
@MainActor @Suite struct ProbeFail { @Test func failedStreamIsReleased() async {
  let counter = ProbeCounter(); var fail: (@MainActor () -> Void)?
  let watches = FolderWatches(.init(watchFolder: { _, _, onFail in counter.live += 1; fail = onFail; return { counter.live -= 1 } },
    readModifiedTime: { _ in 1 }, onFolderChanged: { _, _ in }, forgetCachedListings: { _, _ in }, scheduler: ManualScheduler()))
  watches.watch(windowID: 1, path: "/tmp/x"); fail?(); watches.checkForMissedChanges(windowID: 1)
  #expect(counter.live == 1) } }
```
Run: `swift test --filter ProbeFail`. Result: `Expectation failed: counter.live == 1 ... streams alive after fail+restart: 2`. The fix is to call the old `watch.stop?()` before setting it to nil in onFail.

Reference: apps/desktop/src/main/bootstrap.ts:181 calls Node's `watch(path, ...)` in the Electron main process. libuv's FSEvents backend does the realpath there, and the stream is created on libuv's own CF-loop thread. The renderer's UI thread never touches the disk. Port: FSEventsFolderWatcher.watchFolder is `@MainActor` and runs `realpath(path, nil)` (line 21), then FSEventStreamCreate/SetDispatchQueue/Start (lines 33-41), on the UI thread. FolderWatches (all @MainActor) calls it from watch() on every folder change, and from checkForMissedChanges() whenever a window comes forward after its watch failed (for example, a share that went away). The preamble says "UI on @MainActor; disk work off the main thread", and PLAN section 4 requires the UI to never block for more than 50 ms. Over a stalled SMB/NFS mount, realpath blocks until the network filesystem times out, which beachballs the window. In the reference only the Electron main process waits. Measured with a scratch test (removed afterwards), `swift test -c release -Xswiftc -enable-testing --filter ReviewWatchMainThreadTests`:
```swift
@MainActor struct ReviewWatchMainThreadTests {
  @Test func watchFolderWorkOnMain() throws {
    let start = ContinuousClock.now
    let stop = FSEventsFolderWatcher.watchFolder(path: NSTemporaryDirectory(), onChange: { _ in }, onFail: {})
    #expect(Thread.isMainThread)   // passes: realpath + FSEventStreamStart ran on main
    print(start.duration(to: .now)); stop?()
  }
}
```
Result: it passes with Thread.isMainThread true, at a mean of 0.37 ms and a worst case of 1.0 ms on a local SSD. The cost is unbounded on network volumes. Fix: do the realpath (and the stream start) off the main actor, then hop back to main for the callbacks only. readModifiedTime already does this through Task.detached in FolderWatchWiring.swift:19.

Reference (copyPasteExecution.ts:1427-1437): when copyMetadata throws on a move, it runs `context.metadataNotCopied.add(sourcePath)` first and only then calls preserveModeIfSupported and preserveTimestampsIfSupported. Either of those may throw (any code except ENOTSUP/EOPNOTSUPP, for example EPERM on a share or a locked folder). Callers swallow that throw with `.catch(() => undefined)` (lines 1057, 1382, 1731), but the source path is already in the set. So metadataKeptMessage (line 2703) still keeps the original folder ("...couldn't all be copied, so the original was kept") rather than calling tryRemoveEmptySourceDirectory.

Port (NativeCopy.swift:113-127): `copied = false` is a local variable that only reaches the caller through `return copied`. When `try preserveMode(...)` or `try preserveTimestamps(...)` throws, apply throws, and that false never reaches the caller. A caller that swallows the error as the original does (`try?`) has nothing telling it to keep the original. The cross-disk move then removes the source folder, and its tags, ACL, custom icon and other xattrs that the copy could not take are lost for good.

Failing test (put in Tests/FTOperationsTests/Native/ReviewFolderMetadataTests.swift and run `swift test --filter ReviewFolderMetadataTests`; it fails with 'Expectation failed: keepOriginal'):
```swift
import Foundation
import Testing
@testable import FTOperations

struct ReviewFolderMetadataTests {
    @Test func aMoveWhoseFallbackThrowsStillSaysTheMetadataWasNotCopied() {
        let refused = NativeError.errno(ENOTSUP, syscall: "copyfile", path: "/s", dest: "/d")
        var keepOriginal = false
        do {
            keepOriginal = !(try FolderMetadata.apply(
                source: "/s", destination: "/d", sourceMode: 0o755, sourceMtimeMs: 1500, moving: true,
                copyMetadata: { _, _ in throw refused },
                chmod: { _, _ in throw NativeError.errno(EPERM, syscall: "chmod", path: "/d") },
                utimes: { _, _, _ in }))
        } catch {
            // swallowed as the original does: nothing says to keep the original
        }
        #expect(keepOriginal)
    }
}
```
Fix: report 'not copied' independently of the fallback throwing. For example, return a result that carries both values, or take an `onNotCopied` callback that runs before the fallback, matching the order in the reference.

Reference, packages/core/src/fs/copyPasteExecution.ts:2928-2929 (e4d0872):
  const pickedPaths = new Set(args.report.nodes.map((node) => node.sourcePath));
  const topLevelItemCount = args.items.filter((item) => pickedPaths.has(item.sourcePath)).length;
This is O(items) with hashed lookups.

Port, ExecutionMoves.swift:305-311:
  var pickedPaths: [Int: [String]] = [:]
  for node in report.nodes { pickedPaths[node.sourcePath.utf8.count, default: []].append(node.sourcePath) }
  let topLevelItemCount = items.filter { item in pickedPaths[item.sourcePath.utf8.count]?.contains { $0.utf8.elementsEqual(item.sourcePath.utf8) } ?? false }.count
It groups paths by UTF-8 length and then scans each group linearly. Picking N files from one folder with names of equal length (IMG_0001.JPG ...) puts all N paths in one group, so this is O(N^2) byte comparisons. It runs on the paste thread before the final event, so the result and the end of the progress window are delayed. NameKeys.Exact (a byte-exact Hashable key, already used everywhere else in this package) would keep the exact-match behavior and be O(N).

